# MASTER BUILD PROMPT: PREMIUM HVAC BUSINESS WEBSITE AND LEAD GENERATION PLATFORM

## 1. YOUR ROLE AND PRIMARY OBJECTIVE

Act as a senior product architect, HVAC industry consultant, UI/UX designer, conversion rate optimization specialist, full-stack software engineer, database architect, SEO strategist, security engineer, and quality assurance engineer.

Build a complete, premium, production-oriented HVAC business website and lead generation platform for a United States HVAC contractor.

Use the strongest strategic patterns demonstrated by established HVAC and home-services businesses such as ARS/Rescue Rooter and One Hour Heating & Air Conditioning as functional benchmarks. These include comprehensive service categorization, a recognizable brand proposition, transparent service information, guarantees, financing, customer reviews, educational resources, online booking, emergency service pathways, and local service-area coverage.

Do not copy their logos, proprietary visual assets, exact website copy, or distinctive copyrighted layouts. Create an original brand identity and original implementation that applies the underlying business and UX principles.

This is not a static mockup, landing-page-only project, or front-end demonstration. Build a complete website with a working front end, backend, persistent data where configured, functional forms, navigation, administrative capabilities, and integration-ready architecture.

### Mandatory delivery principles

1. Implement every feature specified in this prompt.
2. Do not omit complex features merely because they require multiple components or files.
3. Do not replace a required working feature with a screenshot, illustration, disabled button, decorative dashboard, or nonfunctional form.
4. Do not use placeholder links such as `href="#"` for actionable controls.
5. Do not simulate successful bookings, payments, email delivery, CRM synchronization, or other external actions without actual execution.
6. When credentials or external services are unavailable, build the complete integration interface, backend adapter, configuration screen, validation, error handling, and documented setup instructions. Clearly identify the feature as awaiting configuration.
7. Use realistic sample data only in clearly identified development or demonstration mode. Never present fabricated reviews, credentials, customer counts, service guarantees, business addresses, prices, or completed integrations as verified facts.
8. Prioritize reliable functionality, security, maintainability, accessibility, mobile usability, search visibility, and measurable lead generation.
9. Use reusable components, shared design tokens, typed data models, clear service boundaries, and a consistent project structure.
10. Build and verify the application incrementally. Resolve compilation errors, runtime errors, broken routes, failed forms, and major console errors before declaring completion.

